			<div class="content" id="post-content-1517975">
									

<p>Okay, ocean makes sense, lots of fish in the sea and all that. Don't know why that wasn't clicking in my head for some reason.</p>
<p>Visibility- Keep in mind that I am no expert here. But it seems to me that a light which does not interact with matter would be exactly as visible from a few feet away as it would be from several thousand miles. If there is no scattering effect from refraction/reflection off of air, there is no reason for this light to ever disipate. It's like it is permanently in a vacumn, and in space astronauts can spot the blinking lights of other man-made objects from hundreds of miles away with the naked eye.</p>
<p>For the worst-case space-launch, I get that moving it away is the primary goal, and that it would come back down eventually makes sense. But what concerns me is that there would be no way to predict <em>where</em> it would come down. Once it gets up there, it becomes a moving, constantly shining light source against the blackness of space. That is going to turn some ehads in the astronomers guild, if not make the national new services. Once all these scientists and amateurs start observing the &quot;newly discovered comet&quot; or whatever they think it is, it gets pulled around like a tug-of-war with hundreds of players. On the other hand, I don't have any suggestions for a better &quot;if all else fails&quot; contingency, so it amounts to nothing.</p>

							</div>

								
					
