			<div class="content" id="post-content-1517985">
									

<p>Visibility - inverse square law. if it were a laser then yes, it'd be (nearly) just as visible. but the light is spreading out in an ever expanding sphere. the rule is that if you double the distance, you divide the amount of light hitting a certain area by 4. triple the distance, divide by 9, etc. the nearest possible observer(given where Containment Area-52 is) would be over 500 miles away. the point of light shines brightly, but measures only .5cm across. i argue that at its current brightness it would be visible from as much as 10 miles away, but 500 miles away only 1/2500 this amount of light would be striking the eye of the observer.</p>
<p>worst case space launch: you've summed up exactly what the foundation's problem is with the scenario, yes. the point is that this will have become the best option, as terrible as it is.</p>

