<p>not quite. let me explain it a bit better. only a certain amount of energy is emitted in any direction. in a laser, all the light is travelling in a straight line together, or at least as straight as the manufacturer is capable of making it. in light emitted in a sphere around an object, the further away from the object, the less light is present in any area of space. it's the same amount of light, but it's spread across a greater surface area.</p>
<p>surface area is A=4πr². a sphere that is, say, two feet wide will have a surface area of about 12.56 sq ft. a sphere that is four feet wide will have a surface area of 50.24 sq ft, exactly 4x as much surface area as the two foot ball. so light that traveled one foot from the light source would be spread across 1/4th as much area as light that traveled two feet from the light source.</p>
<p>if it's the same amount of light, then how can it illuminate four times as much area equally? it cant. thus 1/4th as much light falls on any equivalent area. and so on to infinity.</p>

