			<div class="content" id="post-content-1518006">
									

<p>Firstly, let me say that I enjoy the concept of the object quite a bit. However, I have a few nitpicks about the physics and math involved.</p>
<p>You have the acceleration listed as 10&#160;km/hr*min. To me, that seems like sloppy science on behalf of the foundation, considering how much conversion you have to go through to put that in a form that's typically used, and actually useful for calculations, such as m/s<sup>2</sup>. To me, it would make more sense for the foundation to put the measurement in a more scientific form, so the people calculating containment don't have to whip out their calculators and do five operations before they know that it's accelerating at around 170&#160;m/s<sup>2</sup>.</p>
<p>Also, it seems you could simply use circular motion to contain this far more easily than currently. A monorail track of around 140&#160;m radius would keep the object continuously accelerating at the rate specified without ever gaining on said monorail. Granted, you'd probably need at least two monorails set up, so you can have one stop for maintenance, but the general idea is there. Heck, you could probably keep it in orbit for an indefinite amount of time by setting up your orbit properly and figuring out how to feed and keep the attention of the D-Class up there.</p>

							</div>

								
					
