<p>i concede that m/s is probably a better choice, yes. however i wanted it clear that it does not always accelerate at the same rate, nor even a constant rate, but rather that that is the fastest it has been observed to accelerate. i could have given an average though i suppose.</p>
<p>second point, i actually considered that first. in fact, that is essentially the purpose of Containment Track A. but what do you do if, against your expectations, it catches it? plus, if it is only pursuing a single monorail it is constantly accelerating, meaning eventually it will travel faster than the monorail. basically in the end i felt that there was far more potential for things to go wrong in keeping it solely on tracks. this method requires maintenance, but has failsafes built in just in case things go wrong. if it unexpectedly catches a D-class, it is led to Track A until things are fixed up again. so long as no more than about 6 of the D-class are caught by it, containment can proceed as planned without interruption.</p>

