														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1518044">
									

<p>But wouldn't it be easier to just state &quot;the object has variable acceleration, but has not been observed to go faster than 170&#160;m/s<sup>2</sup>&quot;? It accomplishes the same goal, except in more scientific terminology. And makes it easier to parse, or would for me.</p>
<p>And I think you're misunderstanding the physics involved. If it's accelerating at 170&#160;m/s<sup>2</sup>, then your biggest worry would actually be it falling behind the monorail, as 170&#160;m/s<sup>2</sup> would just be enough acceleration for it to keep up with the monorail. See, when an object is going in a circle, it has to apply acceleration inwards to maintain the circular motion. And if it catches up to the D-Class&#8230; well, that's why you have secondary, smaller tracks calculated against it's base speed + 22.7% (and that plus 22.7%, and that plus 22.7% and so on and so forth). Since its maximum acceleration doesn't change, you'd actually need to go in a smaller circle to maintain distance from it. Basically, by going in a circle of the appropriate radius, you'd be able to negate its acceleration easily, much the same way you are now by making it change direction, except instead of sending it back and forth like a ping pong ball, you'd be leading it in a circle.</p>

